<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Saxony</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1952 CE · capital Dresden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Republican Saxony between the monarchy and East German centralisation, an industrial state of left-wing politics and later Soviet rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-iii-of-saxony">King Frederick Augustus III</a> abdicated in November 1918, and workers' and soldiers' councils briefly took power. A Landtag was elected in 1919, and in 1920 a constitution established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> as a free state within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony's</a> industry made it a stronghold of the Social Democrats and Communists. In 1923 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-zeigner">Erich Zeigner</a>, including Communists, was deposed by the federal government with troops. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> took control in 1933, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-mutschmann">Martin Mutschmann</a> as Reich governor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> was devastated by Allied bombing in February 1945, and Saxony fell in the Soviet occupation zone.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was restored as a Land in the Soviet zone and in 1949 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. In 1952 the GDR abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-of-germany">Länder</a>, dividing Saxony into the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a> (renamed Karl-Marx-Stadt in 1953); it was re-established in 1990.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Free State of Saxony" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxony.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1952 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dresden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Erich Zeigner, Martin Mutschmann</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freistaat Sachsen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=free-state-of-saxony" aria-label="Free State of Saxony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4992 408 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Saxony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5312 664L5312 664M5312 664L5300 622M5312 664L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5312 664h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dresden. <a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=free-state-of-saxony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
